#: ../../../users/releases.rst:55
msgid "Schedule"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/releases.rst:57
msgid ""
"Barring blocking issues, stable versions are released *on the first Tuesday "
"of the month*. A new candidate releases is made *on the second Tuesday of "
"the month*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/releases.rst:62
msgid "How to Choose"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/releases.rst:65
msgid "Built-in / GitHub"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/releases.rst:67
msgid ""
"For releases obtained from Syncthing.net or GitHub, with built-in upgrade "
"functionality, the choice is made in the \"Settings\" dialog. Set the "
"\"Automatic upgrade\" drop down to either \"Stable releases only\" or "
"\"Stable releases and release candidates\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/releases.rst:73
msgid "APT (Debian)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/releases.rst:75
msgid ""
"The choice between stable and candidate is done in the APT source "
"configuration. Please see `our APT instructions <https://apt.syncthing.net/"
">`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/releases.rst:80
msgid "Docker"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/releases.rst:82
msgid ""
"Docker images are pushed to several tags. By pulling a specific tag you can "
"select the release channel you want:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/releases.rst:85
msgid ""
"``syncthing/syncthing:latest`` points to the latest stable release, e.g. "
"``v1.9.0``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/releases.rst:86
msgid ""
"``syncthing/syncthing:rc`` points to the latest release candidate, e.g. "
"``v1.10.0-rc.3``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/releases.rst:87
msgid ""
"``syncthing/syncthing:nightly`` points to the latest nightly development "
"build, e.g. ``v1.10.0-rc.3.dev.1.gd2e32957``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/releases.rst:89
msgid ""
"There are also tags for the major and minor versions, for example "
"``syncthing/syncthing:1.9`` for the latest stable 1.9 patch release and "
"``syncthing/syncthing:1`` for the latest stable release with major version "
"one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/releases.rst:95
msgid "Some Other Distribution Channel"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/releases.rst:97
msgid ""
"If you are getting packages from your Linux distribution, NAS vendor, etc., "
"then you should be getting the *stable* channel. If you get a release "
"candidate you should complain to your distributor or vendor and refer them "
"to this page."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/releases.rst:103
msgid "Nightly Builds"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/releases.rst:105
msgid ""
"It's also possible to run the nightly development builds. These are not "
"releases in any sense of the word, they are simply builds of the current "
"``main`` branch of the repository. These builds are not supported and may "
"contain functionality that is changed or removed before the actual release."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/releases.rst:110
msgid "To use nightly releases, in Advanced Settings -> Options,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/releases.rst:112
msgid ""
"Change \"Releases URL\" to ``https://upgrades.syncthing.net/nightly.json``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/releases.rst:113
msgid "Enable \"Upgrade To Pre Releases\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/releases.rst:115
msgid ""
"Restart Syncthing and it will upgrade to the latest nightly. New nightlies "
"are published at midnight UTC."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/releases.rst:119
msgid "FAQ"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/releases.rst:122
msgid "What's the relationship between candidate and release exactly?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/releases.rst:124
msgid ""
"Every new feature and bugfix begins its life in the development branch, "
"``main``. Once a month the current ``main`` becomes a *release candidate*. "
"This version is identified by \"-rc\" in its name, for example ``v1.5.0-"
"rc.1``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/releases.rst:129
msgid ""
"Those running the candidate channel will update to this release candidate. "
"For the next three weeks it is tested \"in the wild\". Any new, serious "
"issues that are discovered are fixed, and new release candidates ``v1.5.0-"
"rc.2`` etc are created as needed. These release candidates do not include "
"any new features or non-essential bugfixes added to ``main`` in the meantime."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/releases.rst:135
msgid ""
"Stable releases are given version numbers without any suffix - ``v1.5.0``. "
"Unless any serious issues were discovered, this release is exactly identical "
"to the \"-rc.1\" release candidate three weeks prior."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/releases.rst:139
msgid ""
"The cycle then restarts one week later with a new release candidate based on "
"the current ``main`` branch."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/releases.rst:143
msgid "Which bugfixes trigger a new release candidate?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/releases.rst:145
msgid ""
"Those that fix a regression since the last release. Lets say the current "
"release is ``v1.5.0``. We release ``v1.5.1-rc.1`` and discover a new problem "
"that is not present in ``v1.5.0``. This gets fixed and we release a new "
"``v1.5.1-rc.2`` candidate. However, if we discover and fix a problem that's "
"been present since ``v1.4.0``, this fix will instead be incorporated in the "
"next regular cycle."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/releases.rst:153
msgid ""
"What's the difference between the latest candidate and the following stable "
"release?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/releases.rst:155
msgid ""
"Nothing. If we release ``v1.5.1-rc.1`` and no serious problems are "
"discovered during the next twelve days, this is the exact software that will "
"become ``v1.5.1`` for general consumption. Since the version number is "
"different it requires a rebuild and the release signatures / hashes are "
"different. If you are on the candidate channel, your Syncthing will "
"\"upgrade\" from ``v1.5.1-rc.1`` to ``v1.5.1`` when we make the release. "
"This is normal."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/releases.rst:162
msgid "---"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/releases.rst:164
msgid ""
"SemVer-*like* because semver is absolutist about what constitutes an API "
"change, in a way that isn't super helpful to the average user of a program "
"like Syncthing."
msgstr ""

